.\" Manual page for bbq-predictor-publish.
.\"
.\" No version number here, for the reason bbq-predictor.1 gives: VERSION
.\" is the one place this project keeps one.
.TH BBQ\-PREDICTOR\-PUBLISH 1 "2026-10-22" "bbq-predictor" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bbq\-predictor\-publish \- fetch the weather bands once and publish them signed
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bbq\-predictor\-publish
.B \-\-dir
.I DIR
.B \-\-signing\-key
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-station
.IR ID ]
.RB [ \-\-geocode
.IR LAT , LON ]
.RB [ \-\-history\-path
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B bbq\-predictor\-publish \-\-generate\-signing\-key
.I FILE
.br
.B bbq\-predictor\-publish
.RB [ \-\-version | \-\-help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bbq\-predictor\-publish
runs one round of the applet's fetch with no window behind it: every
band from every provider, archived, and then written as the station's
signed files under
.I DIR
for
.BR bbq\-predictor\-serve (8)
to serve. Clients read those files first and fetch from the providers
themselves only when the server has been gone a while.
.PP
It is built with QtCore, QtNetwork and QtSql and nothing that draws, so
a server needs neither a display nor the desktop's libraries to run it.
.PP
What a round writes under
.IR DIR :
a file per month of observations, an index of them, the bands this round
fetched, and a
.I stations
manifest written last. Each file is written aside and renamed into
place, a month whose rows have not changed is not rewritten, and a round
in which no band arrived leaves the last bands in place.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-dir " DIR"
The published tree. Created if missing.
.TP
.BI \-\-signing\-key " FILE"
The Ed25519 key that signs what is written: its 32\-byte seed and
nothing else. Refused if any other user can read it, and loaded before
anything is fetched, so a bad key costs a message and not a round.
.TP
.BI \-\-station " ID"
The station to fetch and publish, for example
.BR ISTOCK877 .
Taken from the applet's settings when omitted.
.TP
.BI \-\-geocode " LAT,LON"
Where the forecast bands are aimed. Derived from the station when
omitted.
.TP
.BI \-\-history\-path " FILE"
The archive to fetch into and publish from, instead of the default one.
.TP
.BI \-\-generate\-signing\-key " FILE"
Create a new signing key at
.IR FILE ,
readable by its owner alone, print its public half in hex, and exit.
Never overwrites an existing file: losing a key strands every client
built with its public half.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
A round distinguishes a partial fetch from a failed one, because a
station going quiet for an hour is ordinary and the other bands still
land in the archive:
.TP
.B 0
Every band answered.
.TP
.B 3
Some band failed, but the composite still covers now. The archive
advanced. The systemd timer forgives this by name.
.TP
.B 1
Nothing covers now: the run stored nothing usable. A publish that fails
after the fetch exits 1 as well.
.TP
.B 2
A configuration error, including a
.B \-\-signing\-key
that cannot be used.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/default/bbq-predictor
Read by the scheduled round.
.B BBQ_STATION
must be set there; unset, the round skips itself and says why rather
than fetching for a station nobody named.
.B BBQ_PUBLISH_DIR
and
.B BBQ_SIGNING_KEY
name the tree and the key, defaulting to the two paths below.
.TP
.I /var/lib/bbq-predictor/signing.key
The key, made once as the account that publishes:
.IP
.nf
sudo \-u bbq\-predictor bbq\-predictor\-publish \e
        \-\-generate\-signing\-key /var/lib/bbq\-predictor/signing.key
.fi
.TP
.I /var/lib/bbq-predictor/published
The tree
.BR bbq\-predictor\-serve (8)
serves.
.TP
.I /var/lib/bbq-predictor/history.sqlite
The archive the rounds build. Readable, so the applet's archive
diagnostic in
.BR bbq\-predictor (1)
can inspect it without root.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/bbq-predictor/fetch
What both schedulers run: the systemd timer
.I bbq\-predictor\-fetch.timer
every five minutes where systemd runs, and
.I /etc/cron.d/bbq\-predictor
everywhere else. It runs as the
.B bbq\-predictor
system account.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bbq\-predictor (1),
.BR bbq\-predictor\-serve (8)
.PP
The project's design record is
.I project.md
in its source tree, which the
.B bbq\-predictor
package installs under
.IR /usr/share/doc/bbq-predictor/ ;
it is authoritative over this page.
